nodecode-vram is a rewrite of jimmy77733's jimmy77733/coffee-vram:
https://github.com/jimmy77733/coffee-vram
commit ce9db238dd51c6713b4fdc1140635ad6b616ee57
MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 JYUN MING CHEN (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the questions the original's `vram_status` tool and its
`/vram` command answered, and the one action `/vram evict` took. The fit
check is the original's estimator (the model's file size from `/api/tags`
plus the KV cache its own `model_info` shape implies, at the context Ollama
will actually load, with the KV-cache type and the safety margin as
settings), its reading of a model already loaded as a measured size, its
hybrid-attention correction, its budget cap (the reserve and the hold line,
whichever is lower), and its eviction plan over idle, unpinned models, the
one nearest its expiry first. `vram_unload` is the `POST
/api/generate {"model": …, "keep_alive": 0}` the original sent, with its
pinned-model and not-loaded refusals. The GPU numbers come from
`nvidia-smi --query-gpu=memory.total,memory.used` when it is installed and
from Ollama's own figures when it is not, which is the original's NVML-then-
`nvidia-smi`-then-nothing fallback with the NVML call left out (a cell may
run a program the machine already has, not open a driver library). The
settings that survived (`ollama_url`, `reserve_mb`, `warn_pct`, `hold_pct`,
`kv_cache_type`, `estimate_overhead_pct`, `gpu_total_mb`, `pinned_models`)
are the section's, in MB rather than fractional GB because a section field is
an integer.

What was left out: the `llm_execution` middleware that held every local call
in a first-come-first-served queue and unloaded a model to make room for it,
and with it the scheduler, the keep-warm `keep_alive` extension, the
`queue_timeout_s` fail-open, the `sample_interval_ms` background sampler and
its in-memory history; the `budget_pool` choice between the GPU carve-out,
system RAM and their sum; the interrupt-flag check; the desktop status chip,
pane, toasts and command palette; and the `hermes coffee-vram` CLI. All of
them are the harness's own surfaces -- a queue around its calls, a chip in
its window, a subcommand of its binary -- or a live loop a question-and-
answer cell has no place for. `clock`, `mode`, `alert_source`,
`on_overflow`, `watch_external`, `evict_idle_s`, `show_*` and
`max_concurrent_local` were the same surfaces' settings and had nothing left
to configure. On this side the model asks before it switches: `vram_fit`
answers "does it fit, and what would make room", and `vram_unload` performs
the eviction the original's queue would have performed on its own.
